From 4191a814c9bc77f40fe7e179e1502c979fc72204 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duck-brain <duck-brain@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 23:34:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] duck-brain P23: torque as the servos have it; a relaxed servo
 gets no goal

Alex's bench, 07.11.2026: torque left on (40 = 1 on all 15) by the servo
tool, robotd started without enable; health said torque None, a relax
answered "torque off", and the servos were stiff again. The relax did
write 40 = 0 - but the loop goes on writing goals, and the STS/HD firmware
switches torque on when it is given a goal.
- The census reads 40 on all 15: robot.health -> feetech.torque (on if any
  is on) and servos[].torque, read at startup and after every verified
  write, never None for a servo that answers.
- A goal goes only to a servo known to hold torque; a dry run journals
  them all as before.
- Torque off (relax, the cutoffs, stopping robotd) always writes 40 = 0 to
  all 15, read back, whatever the driver believes; stopping does it once
  the bus has been up, never in a dry run.
- torque_on takes torque off again when a step after the goal fails: the
  goal alone has switched it on.
- The emulators (feetech::emu and duck-brain's scripts/feetech_emu.py)
  switch torque on at a goal write, as the servos do.
- A robotd test: the host IMU sample reaches robot.state on the Feetech
  bus - quaternion and gyro in imu, gravity and the fall verdict in safety.
